  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: The Landlord may enter the unit at any time and for any reason without notice to the Tenant.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:10:46.057590Z",
 "text": "No, this clause is not enforceable.\n\nUnder **section 25** of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), a landlord may enter a rental unit *only* in accordance with section 26 or section 27:\n* **Section 26** allows entry without written notice only in strictly limited circumstances—such as an emergency, when the tenant consents *at the time of entry* (advance blanket consent in a lease does not qualify), for agreed regular housekeeping, or to show the unit to prospective tenants after a notice of termination has been given.\n* **Section 27** requires at least 24 hours' written notice, entry between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m., and a specific statutory reason for entry.\n\nBecause this clause purports to allow the landlord to enter outside the exhaustive rules mandated by section 25, it conflicts with the Act and is void under **section 4(1)** of the RTA.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 25",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
